<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ernest II, Duke of Saxe-Gotha-Altenburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1745">1745</a>–<a href="/years/1804">1804</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ernest II was Duke of Saxe-Gotha-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a> from 1772 and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> ruler. A patron of science, he founded the Seeberg observatory near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>, which became a leading centre of astronomy under Franz Xaver von Zach. He also supported education and the arts and was a member of the Illuminati.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ernest-ii-duke-of-saxe-gotha-altenburg.jpg" alt="Ernest II, Duke of Saxe-Gotha-Altenburg" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Jonas Michael († 1791) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ernst-II_1775.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ernest was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> on 30 January 1745, the third but second surviving son of Frederick III, Duke of Saxe-Gotha-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a>, and Luise Dorothea of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>. The death of his elder brother Frederick in 1756 made him heir to the duchy. His mother took great care over the training of Ernest and his younger brother August and had them taught by a select group of teachers. In 1768 and 1769 the two princes travelled through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where Ernest met leading figures in politics, science and the arts. In 1769 he married Princess Charlotte of Saxe-Meiningen; they had four sons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ernest succeeded his father in 1772. He promoted schools, the economy, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, art collections, libraries and the natural sciences, and his duchy came to rank first among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> duchies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. His private interests were astronomy and physics. He named the clockmaker Johann Andreas Klindworth court mechanic and brought the astronomer Franz Xaver von Zach to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>, where together they founded the observatory that became a European centre of astronomy. Gotha drew visitors of note, among them Goethe, who came several times. Under the influence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> he thought of abdicating and emigrating, had land bought near Montgomery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> and in 1796 commissioned Joseph Ramée to design a country house, but he remained in Gotha.</p><p>A cousin of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> through his aunt's marriage to Frederick, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, he was made a knight of the Order of the Garter in 1790. He became a Freemason in 1774 and Grand Master of the Landesloge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1775. In 1783 he joined the Bavarian Illuminati under the name Quintus Severus or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timoleon">Timoleon</a>, and in 1787 he gave its founder, Adam Weishaupt, asylum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ernest died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> on 20 April 1804 and was buried wrapped in a white cloth on the island in the palace park. His will asked that the observatory survive as the only visible sign of his existence. His subjects regarded him as a man with magical powers, and a legend still told in Gotha says that he could banish fire. After his death the Garter insignia were handed back to the order.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ernest II of Saxe-Gotha-Altenburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ernest II</b>, in the 18th century, was an Enlightenment patron of astronomy who founded the Seeberg observatory.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-gotha">Duchy of Saxe-Gotha</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.95,10.72&amp;year=1783" aria-label="Gotha on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ernest II, Duke of Saxe-Gotha-Altenburg"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5574 5724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5574 5724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.95,10.72&amp;year=1783">What was here in 1783? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
